WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.542
CARTER MORGAN: Did
you hear the news?

00:00:01.542 --> 00:00:03.360
I heard that Critter
Junction got a fever,

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.963
and the only prescription
is more services.

00:00:05.963 --> 00:00:07.380
Let's take a look
at how we helped

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.713
Critter Junction turn their
monolith into microservices.

00:00:09.713 --> 00:00:12.948
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.887 --> 00:00:18.470
While much of Critter
Junction's focus

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:21.750
has been on redesigning existing
systems to be more scalable,

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.370
they also had to have
a plan for new systems.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.120
They wanted to add a
level editor feature,

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.707
and instead of using C++
like the main game engine,

00:00:28.707 --> 00:00:31.040
they wanted to write it in a
designer-friendly scripting

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.557
language like Python.

00:00:32.557 --> 00:00:34.640
This led them to wonder
if it would be a good idea

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.360
to break out all of
their core services

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.990
into independent
services, and if so, how

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:40.670
should they go about it?

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.010
When our team first took a
look at Critter Junction's

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.320
architecture, we
told them the truth.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.030
Not everything needs
to be a microservice.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.240
Microservice apps add
complexity because how

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.740
the independent services
communicate can be non-obvious.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.160
And that communication
happens over a network,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.490
so message passing and
security can become a concern.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:00.620
Plus, just by better
architecting your monolith

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.894
to include monitoring, logging,
CI/CD, and health checks,

00:01:03.894 --> 00:01:05.269
you can get a lot
of the benefits

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:06.860
of a microservice-based
approach,

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.620
like observability
and recoverability.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.020
For their level editor
feature, Critter Junction

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.660
needed the ability to deploy
services independently

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.490
and for separate teams
to write components

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.120
in different languages.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.390
So for their needs,
a microservice or

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.060
service-oriented architecture
gave them the most flexibility.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.060
We thought this would be
pretty straightforward,

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.980
but we ran into a problem.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.590
While some of their services
didn't require state,

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.533
some of the services
clearly did,

00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:34.700
and some services were
tightly coupled in a way that

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.610
made it unclear if they
actually required state or not.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:40.730
Ideally, you want your services
to be stateless and not retain

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.590
any local persistent data.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.300
This way, each
request or interaction

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.822
can be handled without needing
to know what happened before.

00:01:47.822 --> 00:01:49.280
This approach makes
it a lot easier

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.870
to scale and recover
your services,

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:53.630
because services can grow,
shrink, or be restarted

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.760
without losing any data.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.470
Luckily, before we started
refactoring the system,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.750
we took the time
to create a plan.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.718
The first step was to take an
inventory of the entire system.

00:02:03.718 --> 00:02:05.510
We spent some time
refactoring the monolith

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.240
to decouple some
of the components.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.699
While this was going
on, the new level editor

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:13.212
was designed and deployed
as a microservice.

00:02:13.212 --> 00:02:15.170
This was great because
it gave Critter Junction

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.600
a preliminary test for the
new approach, where they could

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.940
set up and test deploying
services independently

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.460
and make sure they can
communicate with each other.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.450
Then we started migrating
the monolith in stages.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.380
Instead of doing
everything at once,

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.303
we started with the stateless
components, like the UI.

00:02:31.303 --> 00:02:32.720
After the stateless
components, we

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.410
went on to migrate staple
services with independent data

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:39.270
sets, like their auth
service, which wasn't as easy.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.590
Finally, we migrated trickier
features with shared data

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.790
sets that we couldn't
quite fully decouple,

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.510
like their shopping
cart feature.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.020
Over time, Critter
Junction can work

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.420
to re-architect these
features, but for now, they

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.490
have a functional and
more easily maintainable

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:55.100
microservice architecture.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.500
Transitioning from a monolith
to microservice approach

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.740
isn't always necessary and
it isn't always effortless,

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:02.690
but in this case, it
gave Critter Junction

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.470
key business
features they needed,

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.240
so it was the right choice.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.710
So that's a wrap for this
episode of "Season of Scale."

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.798
Stay tuned to learn how
Critter Junction continues

00:03:11.798 --> 00:03:14.090
to improve the design of
their app architecture and dev

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.140
processes.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.170
And, remember, always
be architecting.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

